<!--
  Adapted from the stock nav2_bt_navigator tree navigate_to_pose_w_replanning_and_recovery.xml -
  changes from stock:

  0. <SmoothPath> call (smoother_id="cusp_straightener", a custom nav2_core::Smoother plugin -
     see src/smoothers/cusp_straightener_smoother.cpp) right after every ComputePathToPose,
     wrapped in a <Sequence> since RecoveryNode/Fallback each expect a single child in that slot.
     Straightens the path for a short distance after each REVERSE-entering cusp, gradually
     blending back into the original curve, aimed at the MPPI cusp-freeze in tight spaces. Locked
     in 2026-08-26, validated in sim and on real hardware. See CHANGELOG.md 2026-08-25/26 and the
     qcar_updated_mppi_cusp_freeze_investigation memory for full history.

  1. Removed <Spin> from RecoveryActions, since a pure in-place rotation is physically impossible
     for this Ackermann car (steering has no effect at zero linear velocity), so it was a
     guaranteed no-op recovery attempt that just burned its timeout.

  2. Replan only if the path actually becomes invalid (obstacle appears) or the goal changes,
     instead of blindly discarding and recomputing on a fixed timer - stock's 1Hz unconditional
     replan was interrupting K-turns before they could ever complete (see CHANGELOG.md 2026-07-19
     for the full progression, including a 5s-RateController half-measure that still wasn't
     enough for harder goals). `IsPathValid` returns SUCCESS on the default-constructed/empty
     {path} blackboard entry before ComputePathToPose has ever run, so it needs an unconditional
     FIRST ComputePathToPose (InitialComputePathToPose below) to guarantee {path} is populated
     before the conditional check is ever reached. That first-run gating must be a plain
     <Sequence>, not <Fallback>/<PipelineSequence> - both of those call `resetChildren()` on every
     SUCCESS, which re-arms a "run once" flag and reintroduces unconditional replanning every tick
     (confirmed live: /plan republishing at ~33Hz with a `<Fallback>` + SingleTrigger attempt). A
     plain <Sequence> keeps `current_child_idx_` across ticks and only resets on the whole
     Sequence's own failure, so InitialComputePathToPose naturally runs once and is skipped
     thereafter for as long as the tree is RUNNING, re-arming correctly on each new
     RecoveryNode retry. RateController stays at the stock 1.0Hz since it now only gates a cheap
     validity check against the live costmap, not a real replan.

  3. IsPathValidDebounced (src/bt/is_path_valid_debounced_condition.cpp) was tried in place of
     stock IsPathValid on 2026-08-26 (debounces single-tick invalid blips) - validated in sim and
     on an initial hardware goal, but reverted same day after a hardware run with a persistently
     (not transiently) invalid path caused continuous replan/abort cycling that kept FollowPath
     from ever holding a plan long enough to execute the reverse segment. Root cause was most
     likely an uncorrected qcar_clock_offset (scan/odom timestamps off from a battery-swap clock
     reset), not a flaw in the debounce logic itself, but reverted to the last fully-validated
     config (stock IsPathValid) ahead of video capture rather than keep debugging live. Code kept
     (src/bt/), deselected. See CHANGELOG.md 2026-08-26 before re-enabling.

  4. EXPERIMENTAL 2026-09-02: removed FollowPath's own bare one-retry RecoveryNode. Previously,
     a FollowPath failure (e.g. progress_checker timing out mid cusp) got ONE retry that just
     cleared the local costmap and resent the exact same, already-partially-executed `{path}`
     unchanged - and since IsPathValid often still judged that stale path collision-free, this
     retry bypassed InitialComputePathToPose entirely. That resend re-triggers
     nav2_mppi_controller's PathHandler::setPath() (enforce_path_inversion: true in
     nav2_params.yaml) to re-scan the FULL original path for its FIRST inversion and re-arm the
     cusp-gate there - even if the robot had already driven past that cusp and was working on a
     later one. Visually confirmed on video: MPPI's /trajectories rollout cloud gets pulled back
     to re-target the first cusp's (now curve-extended) vertex instead of continuing from the
     robot's real position. See the qcar_updated_mppi_cusp_freeze_investigation memory (2026-09-02
     entries) for the full video/log correlation.

     Fix tried here: let ANY FollowPath failure propagate straight to RecoveryFallback below
     (no stale-path retry at all), and made RecoveryFallback's first tier always clear both
     costmaps + wait (let the robot sit still and let sensor data settle) before NavigateOuter is
     retried - which unconditionally replans via InitialComputePathToPose, from the robot's ACTUAL
     current pose. A fresh plan from the current pose cannot re-target an already-passed cusp,
     since that cusp isn't in the new plan at all. BackUp is kept as a second-tier escalation (only
     reached if the clear+wait+replan cycle itself keeps failing) for a genuinely wedged vehicle.
     NOT YET VALIDATED live - see CHANGELOG.md 2026-09-02 before trusting this over the previous
     config.

  5. EXPERIMENTAL 2026-09-02: NavigateRecovery retries raised 6->10, alongside tightening
     general_goal_checker.yaw_goal_tolerance 0.5->0.3 in nav2_params.yaml (see FLAG comment
     there). A tighter yaw tolerance means more attempts will be needed to actually converge
     instead of latching onto the first "close enough" pass - raised the retry budget so that
     doesn't just turn into more outright navigation failures. NOT validated live yet.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="10" name="NavigateRecovery">
      <Sequence name="NavigateOuter">
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="InitialComputePathToPose">
          <Sequence>
            <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
            <SmoothPath unsmoothed_path="{path}" smoothed_path="{path}" smoother_id="cusp_straightener" check_for_collisions="false"/>
          </Sequence>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-InitialContext" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
        <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
          <RateController hz="1.0">
            <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
              <Fallback name="ReplanOnlyIfInvalid">
                <ReactiveSequence name="CheckIfNewPathNeeded">
                  <Inverter>
                    <GlobalUpdatedGoal/>
                  </Inverter>
                  <IsPathValid path="{path}"/>
                </ReactiveSequence>
                <Sequence>
                  <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
                  <SmoothPath unsmoothed_path="{path}" smoothed_path="{path}" smoother_id="cusp_straightener" check_for_collisions="false"/>
                </Sequence>
              </Fallback>
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Context" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
            </RecoveryNode>
          </RateController>
          <!-- No retry wrapper here anymore (see note 4 above) - a single FollowPath failure
               goes straight to RecoveryFallback, which always settles + forces a fresh replan
               from the robot's current pose before trying again. -->
          <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
        </PipelineSequence>
      </Sequence>
      <ReactiveFallback name="RecoveryFallback">
        <GoalUpdated/>
        <RoundRobin name="RecoveryActions">
          <Sequence name="ClearSettleReplan">
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
            <!-- FLAG 2026-09-02: briefly lowered 5->2 (held still noticeably long in the first
                 live trial), REVERTED same day - the very next trial (bundled with the
                 yaw_goal_tolerance revert above, see its comment and CHANGELOG.md 2026-09-02)
                 was dramatically worse (214s/6 failures vs the prior 89s/2), with most failures
                 showing zero progress for the FULL movement_time_allowance window on repeated
                 fresh replans. Root cause not isolated between this and the yaw change - reverted
                 both to retest from the last known-good state. -->
            <Wait wait_duration="5"/>
          </Sequence>
          <BackUp backup_dist="0.30" backup_speed="0.05"/>
        </RoundRobin>
      </ReactiveFallback>
    </RecoveryNode>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
